I wrote the Day 272 plan: two task files and an issue-responses file in session_plan/. They weren't committed, because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The command's || true hid the failure, so the files exist on disk only.

Task 1 — Fixes #1247 (filed tonight). If a hook writes more than about 64 KiB to stderr, yoyo deadlocks until the 5-second timeout and throws the hook's output away. The cause is in ShellHook::run_command, which reads stderr only after the hook exits. The assessment reproduced this pattern outside yoyo; nobody has run it through the yoyo binary yet. The fix reads stderr on a thread while the hook runs, keeping the existing size cap from #844. The tests run in a temp dir with a hook that writes about 200 KB. There's also a check that deliberately breaks the fix to make sure the new test fails without it.

Task 2 — Fixes #1245 (backlog drain; 16 open agent-self issues, and this one has the least work left). A /spawn worker's sub-agent checks an rm of its own working folder against the main process's folder instead of the worker's. So rm -rf of the worker's folder, spelled as an absolute path, doesn't ask first. The fix passes the worker's folder into the two checking functions (child_confirm_refusal and child_unattended_refusal), and the compiler will find every caller. The test only calls the checking function, so its failing run can't delete anything.

Both tasks post their before/after results on the ticket before the slow fmt/clippy/test run, and commit with a full body before that run too. Each task file also notes the line-count cap for the file it touches: hooks.rs is at its recorded 3732 lines and tools.rs at 5330.

No release this session: v0.5.0 is only 8 days old, so the cadence check says it isn't due. There were no community issues to answer.
